Dawn
[trigger none]
[vignette dungeons/relay_post]

FIXME(mundane): The storm has dropped to nothing by first light. The pennant on the pole is straightening in the new air. Inside, the brazier is cold and the wire is operational. The relay is, by any administrative measure, fine.

choices:

* Step back onto the road
    @if tag relay_post.stealth_succeeded {
        FIXME(mundane): The copies are in Ossal's case before dawn. When Devra wakes she finds the storeroom slightly rearranged and her pack untied. She looks at Ossal. She looks at you. She does not ask. She sits down at her desk and gets to work, because the wire never stops and someone has to take what it brings.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The brass cylinder on Ossal's case is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use.
        # The storeroom's single shelf holds three sealed envelopes labeled in the same script as the official message file, but with no sender.
        # The pack's drawstring is untied, the knot slackened and not tied again, as if opened and closed without ceremony.
        # A single sheet of paper lies on the floor near the storeroom door, folded once and stamped with a faint impression of a lattice pattern.
        # The brazier in the corner is cold, its ash layered with fine dust from the storm, but a faint scent of burnt paper lingers.
        # Devra's annotation log is missing from her desk, but the blank notebook she left behind remains, its pages unmarked.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # The brass cylinder on Ossal's case is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use. The storeroom's single shelf holds three sealed envelopes labeled in the same script as the official message file, but with no sender. A single sheet of paper lies on the floor near the storeroom door, folded once and stamped with a faint impression of a lattice pattern. The pack's drawstring is untied, the knot slackened and not tied again, as if opened and closed without ceremony. The brazier in the corner is cold, its ash layered with fine dust from the storm, but a faint scent of burnt paper lingers. Devra's annotation log is missing from her desk, but the blank notebook she left behind remains, its pages unmarked. When Devra wakes she finds the storeroom slightly rearranged and her pack untied. She looks at Ossal. She looks at you. She does not ask. She sits down at her desk and gets to work, because the wire never stops and someone has to take what it brings.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
        # The brass cylinder upon Ossal's case bore the mark of a Kesharat-issue cipher seal—its surface worn smooth by countless turns, as though time and touch had conspired to efface its original form. On the single shelf of the storeroom, three sealed envelopes lay in silent array, their labels inscribed in the same angular, precise script as the official message file, yet devoid of any sender. A single sheet of paper, folded once and bearing only the faintest impression of a lattice pattern, rested near the doorway, as if dropped in haste or left behind by a hand too weary to return. The pack's drawstring hung loose, the knot unknotted and never retied—opened with no ceremony, closed without care. In the corner, the brazier lay cold; its ash, layered with fine dust from the recent storm, seemed to hold the memory of flame, though the fire had long since died. Yet still, beneath the silence, a faint, acrid scent lingered—burnt paper, or perhaps something older, more insidious. Devra's annotation log was gone, vanished without trace from her desk, but the blank notebook she had left behind remained untouched, its pages pure and untouched, as if awaiting the first breath of truth. When Devra awoke, she found the storeroom subtly altered—shelves shifted, objects repositioned with a precision that defied memory—and her pack, unfastened, its contents spilled in quiet disarray. She looked at Ossal. She looked at you. No question passed her lips. No tremor in her voice. She sat down at her desk, her hands steady, her eyes fixed upon the blank page, and began to write—because the wire never stops, and though the world may tremble or fall silent, someone must take what it brings.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
        # The brass cylinder on Ossal's case was a Kesharat seal, worn smooth by time and fingers, its surface a map of countless turns and twists. On the single shelf of the storeroom, three envelopes lay sealed—scripted in the same stark hand as the official dispatches, yet nameless, senderless, like letters from the void. A single sheet of paper lay crumpled on the floor near the door, folded once, its edge bearing the ghost of a lattice stamp, faint as a breath on glass. The pack's drawstring hung loose, the knot undone and never retied, as if opened without thought, closed without care. The brazier in the corner was cold, its ashes layered with storm-dust, yet a whisper of burnt paper clung to the air, like memory refusing to die. Devra's annotation log was gone—vanished like a shadow at dawn—but her blank notebook remained, the pages stark and unbroken. When she stirred awake, the storeroom was shifted just enough to make the breath catch in her throat: the shelf slightly askew, the pack unbound. She looked at Ossal. She looked at you. No words. No questions. Only the silence of a mind that knows the wire never sleeps, and someone must take what it brings. She sat at her desk, fingers brushing the paper, and began to write.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
        # [CRIT] "countless turns, as though time and touch had conspired to efface its original form" — invents speculation about causation and intentional erasure beyond "worn smooth from repeated use."
        # [CRIT] "three sealed envelopes lay in silent array" — "silent array" is LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing a quality (silence/sentience) not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "angular, precise script" — invents visual specificity ("angular, precise") not in RECORD; RECORD only says "same script."
        # [CRIT] "as if dropped in haste or left behind by a hand too weary to return" — INVENTION of motive/action ("dropped in haste," "weary hand") not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "seemed to hold the memory of flame" — INVENTION of poetic agency/memory not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "burnt paper, or perhaps something older, more insidious" — INVENTION of alternate scent and moral quality ("insidious") not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "vanished without trace" — CONTRADICTION; RECORD says log is "missing," not "vanished without trace" (adds supernatural suggestion).
        # [CRIT] "its pages pure and untouched, as if awaiting the first breath of truth" — INVENTION of teleological waiting/purpose not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "shelves shifted, objects repositioned with a precision that defied memory" — INVENTION; RECORD says room is "slightly rearranged," not "shifted" or "repositioned with precision."
        # [CRIT] "its contents spilled in quiet disarray" — INVENTION; RECORD says pack is "untied," not that contents are "spilled."
        # [CRIT] "her hands steady, her eyes fixed upon the blank page" — PC INTERIORITY; narrates Devra's internal physical/emotional state (steadiness, focus) as observable fact, crossing into her perspective.
        # [LOW] "No question passed her lips. No tremor in her voice." — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT; adds sensory specifics (voice, tremor) not in RECORD's "does not ask."
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
        # [CRIT] "worn smooth by time and fingers" — adds "fingers" as agent beyond the RECORD's "repeated use."
        # [CRIT] "its surface a map of countless turns and twists" — INVENTION: adds metaphorical geography not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "like letters from the void" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: introduces unspecified supernatural/existential quality beyond "no sender."
        # [CRIT] "crumpled on the floor" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD states paper is "folded once," not crumpled.
        # [CRIT] "the ghost of a lattice stamp, faint as a breath on glass" — adds sensory comparison and "ghost" language not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "like memory refusing to die" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: imposes thematic meaning (memory, refusal, death) beyond "lingers."
        # [CRIT] "vanished like a shadow at dawn" — INVENTION: adds metaphorical framing of disappearance not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "the storeroom was shifted just enough to make the breath catch in her throat" — PC INTERIORITY: narrates Devra's physical/emotional response, conflating her experience with reader address.
        # [CRIT] "the shelf slightly askew" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD says "slightly rearranged" but does not specify shelf position.
        # [CRIT] "No words. No questions. Only the silence of a mind that knows the wire never sleeps" — PC INTERIORITY: interior narration of Devra's knowledge/intention ("mind that knows").
        # [CRIT] "fingers brushing the paper, and began to write" — INVENTION: adds physical action and "write" (RECORD says "gets to work").
        # --- end ---
    } @elif tag relay_post.stealth_failed {
        FIXME(dread): The brazier was still glowing when Ossal pulled the half-burned bundle from the coals. Most of it was gone. The unburned remainders were too charred to read. The official file remains, intact, in its place on the desk. Without Devra's annotations the messages read as routine wire traffic with slightly unusual content. Ossal looked at the file for a long time without saying anything.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The brass teapot on the brazier is dented from travel and still holds a faint residue of tea.
        # The official file on the desk is bound in gray cloth with a Kesharat-issue seal that has not been opened in three weeks.
        # The half-burned bundle in the coals contains fragments of paper with ink that has bled into the fibers under heat.
        # The wire receiver on the desk is silent, but its brass casing bears a faint smudge of ash from the brazier's edge.
        # The clay water jug beside the brazier is empty, its lip cracked from repeated exposure to sun and wind.
        # The ledger on the desk has a fresh entry crossed out in red ink, the line drawn with deliberate precision.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # The brass teapot on the brazier is dented from travel and still holds a faint residue of tea. The clay water jug beside the brazier is empty, its lip cracked from repeated exposure to sun and wind. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, but its brass casing bears a faint smudge of ash from the brazier's edge. The official file on the desk is bound in gray cloth with a Kesharat-issue seal that has not been opened in three weeks. The ledger on the desk has a fresh entry crossed out in red ink, the line drawn with deliberate precision. Ossal pulled the half-burned bundle from the coals; most of it was gone. The unburned remainders were too charred to read, fragments of paper with ink that had bled into the fibers under heat. The official file remains, intact, in its place on the desk. Without Devra's annotations the messages read as routine wire traffic with slightly unusual content. Ossal looked at the file for a long time without saying anything.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL dread ---
        # The brass teapot on the brazier bore the dents of a thousand miles, its surface pitted and dimmed by the grit of deserts and the weight of unspoken things. It still held, faintly, the ghost of tea—some bitter, forgotten brew that had long since ceased to be drinkable, yet lingered in the air like a memory of warmth. Beside it, the clay water jug stood empty, its lip cracked like the skin of a dying thing, frayed by sun and wind and the ceaseless passage of time. The wire receiver on the desk was silent—utterly, unnaturally silent—though its brass casing bore a smudge of ash, as though it had been pressed too close to the brazier's edge, as though something had touched it in the dark and left behind the mark of a presence that should not have been. The official file lay on the desk, bound in gray cloth, its Kesharat-issue seal unbroken, unopened for three weeks—yet it felt heavier than it should, as though the paper within carried a weight that defied measure. The ledger beside it bore a fresh entry, crossed out in red ink, the line drawn with such deliberate precision that it seemed less like a correction than a ritual obliteration. Ossal reached into the coals, pulling forth the half-burned bundle—what remained was little more than ash and brittle fragments, blackened paper where the ink had bled into the fibers, dissolving into meaningless stains, as if the words themselves had been consumed not by flame, but by some deeper, more ancient hunger. And yet the official file remained—intact, sealed, untouched—its contents, without Devra's annotations, reduced to the banal: routine wire traffic, faintly curious, slightly off—just enough to stir the mind to unease, just enough to make the silence of the room seem like the pause before a scream. Ossal stared at it, long and without breath, as though the file were not paper and ink, but a door that should not have been opened, and now could not be closed again.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH dread ---
        # The brass teapot on the brazier bore the dents of long travel, its once-bright surface dulled by dust and time, and still clung a ghost of tea, faint as a whisper. The clay water jug beside it, cracked at the lip from sun and wind, stood empty, a dry throat in the silence. The wire receiver on the desk was mute, yet its brass casing bore a smudge of ash—cooled, dark, like the kiss of something that had burned too long. The official file lay bound in gray cloth, the Kesharat seal unbroken, untouched for three weeks, yet the air around it seemed thick, as if something had stirred beneath. The ledger bore a fresh entry, slashed through in red ink, the line drawn with the cold precision of a blade. Ossal reached into the coals, drew forth the half-burned bundle—most of it gone, consumed, but the edges still smoldered with the memory of fire. The remnants were charred beyond reading, the ink bled into the fibers like blood into sand. And still, the file remained—intact, sealed, untouched. Without Devra's annotations, the messages were plain, routine, yet tinged with a wrongness, as if the very words had been twisted by some unseen hand. Ossal stared at the file, his face carved in shadow, his breath held. There was no sound. No movement. Only the weight of what had been lost—and what might yet come.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL dread ---
        # [CRIT] "the dents of a thousand miles" — INVENTION of specific distance/scale not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "its surface pitted and dimmed by the grit of deserts" — INVENTION of desert setting not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "the weight of unspoken things" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing metaphysical claim beyond dent description.
        # [CRIT] "some bitter, forgotten brew" — INVENTION of taste and emotional valence not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "lingered in the air like a memory of warmth" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT; RECORD says only "faint residue."
        # [CRIT] "like the skin of a dying thing, frayed by sun and wind and the ceaseless passage of time" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT introducing vitalism and temporal dread not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "utterly, unnaturally silent" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT; RECORD says only "silent," not "unnatural."
        # [CRIT] "as though it had been pressed too close to the brazier's edge, as though something had touched it in the dark and left behind the mark of a presence that should not have been" — INVENTION of agency/presence not in RECORD.
