    FIXME(dread): Devra is at her post now, doing the work with the particular precision of someone who has stopped believing in it.

    FIXME(mundane): Ossal sits at the small table by the brazier and writes his inspection report. Post operational. Personnel stable. No anomalies. He believes this. The report is accurate in every particular and false in every way that matters.

    FIXME(mundane): You take your pack and step out into the cleared morning. The mesa is bright and quiet. Behind you the relay carries on with its work, which it will continue to do for as long as the wire continues to bring it work.
    # --- COLOR ---
    # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated handling.
    # The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, the dregs dark and bitter at the bottom.
    # The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its indicator light off, though the lead remains connected.
    # One of the filed transcriptions on the desk has a corner folded down, marked with a pencil note in Devra's hand: "Not from capital."
    # The storm's grit has settled in a fine layer across the stone floor, visible only where the morning light slants through the door.
    # The Kesharat pennant on the pole outside is limp, its fabric stiff with dried dust and salt.
    # --- end ---
    # --- FACTUAL ---
    # You take your pack and step out into the cleared morning. The mesa is bright and quiet. The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated handling. The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, the dregs dark and bitter at the bottom. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its indicator light off, though the lead remains connected. One of the filed transcriptions on the desk has a corner folded down, marked with a pencil note in Devra's hand: "Not from capital." The storm's grit has settled in a fine layer across the stone floor, visible only where the morning light slants through the door. The Kesharat pennant on the pole outside is limp, its fabric stiff with dried dust and salt. Behind you the relay carries on with its work, which it will continue to do for as long as the wire continues to bring it work.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # You take your pack and step out into the cleared morning, where the mesa lies bathed in a pale, brittle light, as if the air itself were thin and strained. The brass cylinder on her desk—the Kesharat-issue cipher seal—gleams with a dull, worn smoothness, its surface worn by countless hands and countless turns, bearing the faint, ghostly imprint of forgotten codes. The pot of tea upon the brazier is cold, the dregs at the bottom black and thick as old blood, tasting of ash and neglect. The wire receiver on the desk lies silent, its indicator light extinguished, yet the lead remains connected, a taut thread of possibility stretched into the void. One of the filed transcriptions lies open, a corner folded down, marked in Devra's hand with a single, stark note: *"Not from capital."* Outside, the storm's grit has settled in a fine, pervasive layer across the stone floor—visible only where the morning light slants through the doorway in thin, dusty beams. The Kesharat pennant on the pole beyond the threshold hangs limp, its fabric stiff and cracked with dried dust and salt, as though the wind had long since abandoned it. Behind you, the relay hums on, a low, persistent thrumming in the walls, carrying out its function with mechanical indifference—so long as the wire brings work, so long as the current holds, it will not cease.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # You heave the pack onto your shoulders and step out into the cleared morning, where the mesa lies bright and still beneath a sky of hammered bronze. The brass cylinder on her desk gleams dully—Kesharat-issue, worn smooth as a river stone from a hundred hands, a seal of authority and silence. The tea on the brazier is cold, the dregs thick and black as old blood at the bottom of the pot. The wire receiver sits mute, its indicator dark, though the lead still snakes away into the wall like a dead serpent. One of the transcribed logs lies open, a corner folded down, a pencil mark in Devra's sharp hand: *"Not from capital."* Outside, the storm's grit lies in a thin, ghostly layer across the stone floor—visible only where the morning sun slants through the door in dusty shafts. The Kesharat pennant on the pole hangs limp, its fabric stiff with dust and salt, a banner of forgotten duty. Behind you, the relay hums on, a quiet, relentless machine, grinding through its work as long as the wire keeps feeding it.
    # --- end ---
